{"type":"text","timestamp":1794340966693,"sessionID":"ses_e38f2e3bfffecd83FRQMpSQm98","part":{"id":"prt_1c70e510b001DlgYnpNfVG7HXl","messageID":"msg_1c70e3db2001wwBwJyCyk3QbXr","sessionID":"ses_e38f2e3bfffecd83FRQMpSQm98","type":"text","text":"<think>npm registry is reachable (tailwindcss 4.3.5). Python 3.13.5 available but Flask not installed globally. Let me create a venv in /workspace/backend/.venv? .dockerignore excludes .venv. I can create a venv at /workspace/.venv (or /tmp/opencode/venv, but /tmp/opencode permission error — maybe owned by root and not writable). Let me check permissions. The error was creating /tmp/opencode/piptest → Permission denied, so /tmp/opencode isn't writable by node user. But I can create venv inside /workspace.\n\nLet me check pip install capability with a venv at /workspace/.venv (gitignored? need to check .gitignore). There's no .gitignore in workspace root. I should create one for .venv? Or place venv in /workspace/backend/.venv (dockerignore excludes **/.venv, and Docker COPY backend/ would copy .venv unless dockerignore excludes it — it does: `**/.venv`). But Docker COPY backend/ backend/ would copy .venv contents if present locally... .dockerignore has `**/.venv` so it's excluded. Good.\n\nActually wait: Docker build context excludes **/.venv so it's fine.\n\nLet me create venv at /workspace/backend/.venv and install flask, gunicorn from requirements (offline cache?). pip has read-only cache access per the prompt. Let me test.\n\nAlso check npm install works for frontend (tailwindcss + @tailwindcss/vite).\n\nLet me do both in parallel.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794340966667,"end":1794340966688}}}
