{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794341367617,"sessionID":"ses_e38f2e3bfffecd83FRQMpSQm98","part":{"id":"prt_1c7146f2d001Cc83guD4wmTzto","messageID":"msg_1c71459fc0017j1L5FHzeulovo","sessionID":"ses_e38f2e3bfffecd83FRQMpSQm98","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794341367618,"sessionID":"ses_e38f2e3bfffecd83FRQMpSQm98","part":{"id":"prt_1c7146f30001AWF3O2OaM4JK5d","messageID":"msg_1c71459fc0017j1L5FHzeulovo","sessionID":"ses_e38f2e3bfffecd83FRQMpSQm98","type":"text","text":"<think>A few issues to fix:\n1. `__import__(\"datetime\").timedelta` — ugly hack. Import timedelta properly.\n2. `parse_pagination` returns tuple of ints or tuple of (Response,int) — the type discrimination is awkward. Let me refactor to return a cleaner result.\n3. The 404 errorhandler conflicts with serve_frontend. Actually I have `@app.get(\"/<path:path>\")` route which handles everything, so 404 handler will rarely trigger except for methods. Let me simplify: remove errorhandler(404) since route handles all paths, but keep api 404 for unknown API routes — actually `/<path:path>` matches `/api/anything` too and returns 404 JSON via my check. Good. But the errorhandler(404) would only fire for methods mismatch (405 actually). Let me remove the 404 handler to avoid confusion but keep the frontend fallback route handling api paths.\n\nWait, there's an issue: `/api/images/<int:image_id>` route for GET, but DELETE on the same. Fine.\n\nAlso `serve_frontend` when DIST doesn't exist (dev mode) → error. Should handle gracefully: return a simple message. Let me make it robust.\n\nLet me also reconsider the pagination helper.\n\nAlso important: the health endpoint and `/api/...` paths — the catch-all `/<path:path>` route is registered but Flask matches more specific rules first. `/api/health` is a static rule; `/<path:path>` is dynamic, so static wins. Good.\n\nLet me rewrite those bits cleanly.\n\nAlso `MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH` — 32MB request limit. Base64 of a 32MB image would exceed. Let me set limit higher, e.g. 64MB? Actually JSON body includes base64 which is ~1.33x. A 413 error for too large is fine. Let me set MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES to 64MB maybe. But then also maybe limit decoded size. Let's keep request max at 64MB and decoded image limit at 32MB.\n\nAlso token TTL check: `tokens.created_at >= now - 30 days`. Since created_at is ISO string with timezone, lexicographic comparison works for same format. Fine.\n\nLet me refine the file.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794341367600,"end":1794341367614}}}
